course: dictionary
lesson: 966
title: Bluebells and context clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bluebell is a wild flower with nodding, bell-shaped blue or purple flowers, and in Britain and Ireland it is a famous sight in spring. If you meet a word you do not know, read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues. «Beech trees make a thick roof of leaves, so little grows beneath them except plants like bluebells that flower in spring.» The thick roof and the lack of plants underneath tell you that bluebells can grow in shade.
explain: Read the whole sentence before you guess. «Bluebells grow from bulbs and flower in April and May, before the trees above are fully in leaf.» A bulb is an underground store that a plant grows from, and the trees not yet in leaf show that the flowers come early, before the leaves block the sunlight.
explain: Always test your meaning. If you think a bluebell is a bird, put that into the sentence about flowers in spring and it stops making sense, so you know to change your idea. A dictionary confirms the meaning when the clues are not enough.
fact: Bluebells are wild plants with nodding, bell-shaped blue or purple flowers.
fact: In Scotland, «bluebell» can also mean the harebell, a delicate blue summer flower.
fact: Bluebell woods are a famous spring sight in Britain and Ireland, and they show that a wood is very old.
fact: It is illegal to dig up bluebells from the wild, so they are protected.
fact: In fairy stories, bluebells ring to call the fairies.
exercise: «Beech trees make a thick roof of leaves, so little grows beneath them.» What does the thick roof tell you about the floor of the wood? | It is a car park | It is covered in snow | *It is shady, so few plants grow there | It is sunny, so many plants grow there
exercise: Lily says a bluebell is a bird because it has the word bell in it. What is a bluebell really? | A small blue bird that sings | A bell that rings in a church | *A plant with bell-shaped flowers | A fairy's house
exercise: «Bluebells are protected: it is illegal to dig them up from the wild.» Find one word in this sentence that means not allowed by the law. | bluebells | wild | protected | *illegal
test: In Scotland, what might the word bluebell mean? | A type of tram | A blue fish that lives in the sea | *The harebell, a delicate blue summer flower | A winter tree with no leaves
test: Why are bluebell woods a sign of an old wood? | Bluebells need a city to grow | *Bluebells grow in ancient woodland, which is very old | Bluebells only grow in new gardens | Bluebells grow only on mountains
exam: Which fact about bluebells is true? | *It is illegal to dig up bluebells from the wild | You can dig up as many bluebells from the wild as you like | Bluebells ring to make the fairies disappear | Bluebells flower only in winter
